================================================================================
Completed (removed from the active list; see git history for full notes)
================================================================================
The Bluetooth stack was moved off the BlueZ userspace tools onto the kernel
mgmt API + raw HCI/L2CAP sockets. Done and verified on-device (2026-07..08):

- LE info scan "Command Disallowed" fixed: replaced hcitool leinfo + dbus
  test-discovery with mgmt Start Discovery (reader thread keeps it continuous)
  and mgmt Add Device auto-connect; LE address-type brute force removed (peer
  type carried on the queue entry).
- Chunker missing-start-block fixes: address-bearing response events made chunk
  starts (HCI 0x05/0x0b/0x0c/0x23; LE meta 0x03/0x04/0x07/0x0a/0x0c/0x14) +
  all-zero BD_ADDR ignored. Multi-unique-address discards no longer observed.
- Event-driven auto-connect: phased SCAN (discovery on, add devices) / CONNECT
  (discovery off, drain mgmt Device Connected/Disconnected/Connect Failed),
  scan-phase pruning, DISCOVERY_OFF_BUDGET bound. Retired the TTL model
  (discovery_wait / drain_auto_connect / sweep_auto_connect / AUTO_CONNECT_TTL /
  discovery_wanted).
- Native L2CAP reachability probe (BlueHydra::L2Ping) replaced the l2ping
  shell-out; validated on-device (reachable -> ACL up ~0.36s; unreachable -> 4s
  timeout -> cancel).
- Classic info/l2ping response times measured; DISCOVERY_OFF_BUDGET = 6s
  confirmed adequate (dominant bounded op is the 4s probe).
- LE remote version interrogation: BlueHydra::HciCommand, a dedicated raw-HCI
  reader thread, issues Read Remote Version Information on each LE Connection
  Complete (the kernel auto-reads LE features but never the version). On-device:
  version reads 1:1 with commands; versions populate in seconds. Also fixed the
  parser Handle+Address drop bug and the LE-version-mislabeled-as-classic bug.
- Pairing/PIN suppression: mgmt Set Bondable(off) + IO Capability(NoInputNoOutput)
  and auto-reject of PIN/confirm/passkey request events.
- Removed the dead bluetoothd/dbus recovery (bluetoothdDbusError, the
  bluetoothd_errors threading) and the dead scanner_status[:test_discovery]
  assignment; hciconfig/bluetoothctl/test-discovery no longer used. README deps
  trimmed accordingly.


================================================================================
Open items
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Add a --test mode / self-check for btmon message receipt and parsing
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Motivation: btmon's output format keeps changing across BlueZ versions
  (combined "Handle: N Address: MAC" lines, added #packet numbers, color codes,
  abbreviated/renamed event headers, moved fields). Each change silently breaks
  a filter/chunk/parse assumption and we notice late. A built-in self-check
  would catch format drift immediately.
- Proposal: a "--test" flag to bin/blue_hydra that runs an end-to-end check
  verifying every expected btmon message type is (a) received, (b) passes the
  enqueue filter, (c) chunked into a single-address chunk, (d) parsed into the
  expected attribute(s). Define a manifest of message types we rely on and the
  attribute each should yield, e.g.:
    * Extended Inquiry Result (0x2f)          -> address, classic_mode, name
    * LE Extended Advertising Report (0x0d)   -> address, le_mode, le_rssi, uuids
    * Read Remote Version Info Complete (0x0c)-> lmp_version
    * Read Remote Supported/Extended Features (0x0b/0x23) -> features_bitmap
    * Remote Name Req Complete (0x07)         -> name
    * Connect Complete (0x03)                 -> address/handle mapping
- Two run modes: fixture mode (feed curated spec/fixtures captures through the
  real BtmonHandler -> Chunker -> Parser and assert the manifest; CI-friendly)
  and live mode (real adapter + known reference devices; document the bench).
- Output: pass/fail per message type, non-zero exit on received-but-unparsed or
  never-received, so it can gate deploys when a new btmon shifts the format.
  Complements the existing chunker/auto-connect health counters (runtime drift)
  by surfacing drift on demand.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Optional: mgmt discovery filter (SetDiscoveryFilter) to cut noise
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Continuous discovery works (reader-thread restart keeps it alive). Refinement:
  set a mgmt discovery filter (RSSI / UUID), the way bluetoothctl does, to cut
  advertising-report volume the pipeline has to process. Low priority; measure
  the processing-speed benefit before implementing.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Better address/handle attribution (resolvable/direct address + handle map)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- (a) Resolvable-private / Direct address handling. The chunker's address
  extraction rejects only "Direct address" lines and the all-zero BD_ADDR. But
  "Local/Peer resolvable private address" and "Direct address" fields can carry
  REAL, non-zero addresses (RPA privacy, directed advertising). Today a non-zero
  RPA is counted as a separate device (-> multi-unique -> discarded) and a real
  Direct address is dropped. Decide, per field, whether it identifies the same
  device (ignore for grouping) or a different one, and whether to record it.
  Needs a capture with non-zero RPA / directed adv to design against.
- (b) Handle map. Some events carry only "Handle: N" (older btmon, or events
  with no inline Address). Address-bearing events are chunk starts on the
  assumption the new format puts "Handle: N Address: MAC" on the line; where an
  event has a handle but NO inline address it becomes a zero-address chunk and
  is dropped. Maintain a handle -> address map (populated from Connect Complete /
  (Enhanced) LE Connection Complete, cleared on Disconnect Complete) so
  handle-only events attribute to the right device. Note: BlueHydra::HciCommand
  already parses the handle out of LE Connection Complete; that parse could feed
  a shared map (also useful for item 4 and item 12).
- Both are correctness/robustness items, most impactful under concurrent
  multi-device processing. Validate on-device with the relevant captures.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Replace the classic info scan (hcitool info) with our own implementation
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Context: the LE info scan is on mgmt Add Device auto-connect and l2ping is the
  native L2CAP probe, so `hcitool info` is the last external info-gathering
  command. mgmt has no non-invasive classic connect-and-read primitive (Pair
  Device bonds), and classic devices do not advertise, so they cannot be
  background auto-connected - the classic path must actively page.
- Goal: read classic remote info ourselves. Reuse the native L2CAP paging: once
  the ACL link is up (mgmt Device Connected, BR/EDR), issue over a raw HCI
  socket the same reads hcitool info performs - Read Remote Version Information
  (0x0c), Read Remote Supported/Extended Features (0x0b/0x23), Remote Name
  Request (0x07) - keyed on the connection handle, then disconnect. btmon already
  captures those events and the parser already handles them; this only changes
  who issues the reads. BlueHydra::HciCommand already does exactly this pattern
  for LE Read Remote Version and is the natural home to extend.
- Open questions (hardware-only): obtaining/tracking the ACL handle (overlaps
  item 3b); whether Read Remote * on a handle we did not create via HCI is
  allowed when the link came up via an L2CAP socket; sequencing so reads finish
  before the link drops. Confirm on a classic device that completes
  name+version+features. If intractable, keep hcitool info as the fallback.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. Remove/replace any remaining external bluez utilities (do it ourselves)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Goal: audit every remaining shell-out to a BlueZ/bluetooth userspace tool and
  replace it with a direct kernel-socket (mgmt / raw HCI / L2CAP) implementation,
  so blue_hydra depends only on the kernel, not the deprecated, version-drifting
  BlueZ tools (a recurring source of parser churn).
- Already removed: test-discovery (dbus), hciconfig, bluetoothctl, l2ping,
  hcitool leinfo.
- Remaining: `hcitool info` (item 4), and any other Command.execute* call sites
  invoking hcitool/btmgmt/etc. Grep the tree and enumerate. Keep bin/rfkill-reset
  (kernel rfkill recovery, not a BlueZ tool). btmon itself stays (it is the
  monitor source). Track each utility as its own sub-item as it is removed.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. Implement GATT service scanning
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Goal: for LE devices, after connecting (mgmt auto-connect path), walk the GATT
  database - primary services, characteristics, descriptors - to enrich device
  fingerprints beyond advertising data + LMP version/features.
- Sketch: over the ATT L2CAP channel (fixed CID 0x0004), issue ATT Read By Group
  Type (primary services), Read By Type (characteristics), Find Information
  (descriptors); collect UUIDs and map well-known 16-bit UUIDs (ties into the
  assigned-16-bit-UUID lookup note up top). Small ATT client on an L2CAP socket
  (own class, reuse the socket-lifecycle patterns; not on Mgmt).
- Coordination: connects LE devices, so it must cooperate with the SCAN/CONNECT
  phasing (connect during a CONNECT window / discovery off) and respect the
  discovery-off budget - a full GATT walk may exceed a version read, so scope how
  much of the DB to fetch per connection / spread across connections.
- Storage: choose which GATT attributes to persist (new Device fields); all
  writes in the result thread (DB rule). Validate on a known GATT peripheral.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. Replace DataMapper with sqlite directly (or a faster maintained gem)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Context: blue_hydra uses DataMapper (long unmaintained) as its ORM over a
  SQLite file; it is a performance/maintenance liability and the single-writer
  result-thread DB rule partly works around its locking.
- Goal: replace with either the sqlite3 gem + hand-written SQL, or a maintained
  toolkit that reads/writes the SAME on-disk SQLite file (Sequel is the strongest
  candidate). MUST preserve the existing file format so existing DBs keep working.
- Scope: enumerate DataMapper usage (BlueHydra::Device + any others, custom
  properties/validations, sync-to-pulse queries, migrations/auto_upgrade); map
  each to the replacement; preserve result-thread-only writes and equivalent
  query behavior (l2ping-candidate query, last_seen windows). Benchmark
  before/after. Large, cross-cutting - schedule alone, gate behind the suite.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. Parallelize classic info scans and l2ping/reachability probes (cap 5 total)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Context: the discovery thread drains the classic info_scan_queue and the
  l2ping_queue strictly one connection at a time, each in a discovery-off window,
  serializing the slowest part of the loop.
- Goal: allow multiple classic connects in flight, hard cap of 5 simultaneous
  operations TOTAL - a single COMBINED limit across classic info scans AND
  reachability probes (at most 5 in flight counting both kinds, NOT 5 of each).
- Sketch: a bounded worker pool pulling from both queues, up to 5 at once. The
  native L2CAP probe is already non-blocking (connect_nonblock + select) so
  probes can be multiplexed on one thread; classic hcitool info (until item 4
  replaces it) is a blocking subprocess needing real threads up to the cap.
- Constraints: DB rule holds (workers only run connects; btmon captures data,
  parsed later in the result thread). With N concurrent ops the discovery-off
  window is bounded by the slowest of the batch, not the sum - re-derive the
  budget accounting for the cap. Must still cooperate with the SCAN/CONNECT
  phasing and disable_scan_before_connect. Cap (5) is a named, tunable, combined
  constant. Use mgmt Device Connected/Disconnected to track completion for early
  per-device teardown. Sequence after items 4 (classic-info rework) land.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9. Success-gate info/leinfo scan timing (don't rate-limit before a success)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- THE GOAL, stated plainly: a device that simply FAILED its connect attempt must
  not then wait the entire info-scan timeout before we try again. info_scan_rate
  is the cadence for re-scanning a device we already have data for. It was never
  meant to be the penalty for one failed connect, and using it as both is the bug.
- Why it matters, measured over a 47 hour run: the gap between consecutive
  failures of the same address was min 571s, median 617s - precisely
  info_scan_rate (600s). Every retry in that run paid the full success cadence.
  Worse, the LE path is dominated by private addresses that rotate (RPA default
  ~15 min), so a 600s wait routinely lands after the address we wanted has
  changed: the "retry" never reached the device at all. Three attempts spaced
  600s apart also meant ~30 minutes to establish that a device was hopeless.
- Problem, mechanically: push_to_queue rate-limits re-scans using
  query_history[mode], stamped when a scan is ENQUEUED (an attempt), not when it
  SUCCEEDS. So an attempt that failed is indistinguishable from one that worked.
- PARTIALLY LANDED. The specific case above is fixed: ConnectTracker::
  FAILED_RETRY_INTERVAL (15s) replaces info_scan_rate in push_to_queue while a
  device is mid-strike-streak (ConnectTracker.retry_soon? - at least one failure,
  not yet struck out). Three strikes now resolve in ~30s instead of ~30 minutes,
  and a retry lands while a private address is still likely valid. Scoped to :le,
  because every strike comes from an LE connect and the tracker is keyed on the
  full address, so without the mode check a dual-mode device's LE failures would
  shorten its classic cadence too - which an LE failure is no evidence for.
  This is only safe because STRIKE_LIMIT bounds it; a short interval with no
  ceiling would spin forever on a device that never answers.
  NB that `mode == :le` check is a stopgap. The intended shape is per-mode
  strikes on their own backoff timer, which removes the special case and gives
  classic the same treatment - see item 13, which now carries that design.
- STILL OPEN, the broader item: gate on last-SUCCESS rather than last-attempt.
  What landed keys off "did the last connect fail", which is not the same as "have
  we ever got data from this device". A device that connects fine but never yields
  a version still looks like a success and still waits the full 600s. Also still
  open: reduce the minimum-interval floor from 45s to 15s (named constant; update
  the validation floor in blue_hydra.rb, currently 45) so the config cannot be set
  below what the failure path now uses.
- Sequencing note: the floor change and the failure backoff stop interacting at
  all once item 13's separate per-mode failure timer lands, because the failure
  path will no longer share query_history or info_scan_rate. Do item 13's timer
  first and this item shrinks to just the last-SUCCESS gating.
- Design: define "success" per mode (leinfo -> lmp_version/features populated;
  classic info -> name/features/version). Known in the RESULT thread when
  parsing; record last-success there in the in-memory query_history (no DB off
  the result thread). Gate becomes: enqueue if (never succeeded AND now -
  last_attempt >= MIN_INFO_SCAN_INTERVAL) OR (now - last_success >=
  info_scan_rate). Interacts with the event-driven flow: a connected-but-no-
  version case should count as not-yet-success and retry sooner.
- BOUNDED BY THE STRIKE RULE, deliberately. ConnectTracker stops attempting an
  address after STRIKE_LIMIT (3) consecutive failures; the streak resets on a
  successful connect and the entry is dropped when the device is marked offline.
  The retry loop this item asks for is therefore capped at 3 attempts while a
  device stays continuously online. That cap is the price of the fast retry above
  - the two were designed together and should not be separated without thought:
    * a device that goes offline and returns starts clean, so intermittent
      devices are unaffected. The case to verify on-device is a device that stays
      ONLINE continuously and never connects: 3 quick attempts, then nothing
      until it times out of online status.
    * if that proves too tight, the options are (a) never-succeeded devices get a
      larger ceiling than devices we already have data for, or (b) the strike
      counter decays with time rather than only on success/offline. Measure
      first - the run that motivated the rule had exactly ONE address of 1329
      that would have hit the ceiling while online, so the ceiling is close to
      free today, and the fast retry made reaching it cheap rather than costly.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10. Lighter kernel auto-connect list clear (mgmt Remove-all + startup clear)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Current: clear_auto_connect empties the list at the end of each CONNECT phase,
  and Runner#stop hci_resets (Set Powered off/on) before closing the mgmt socket,
  which clears the kernel auto-connect list and drops connections on clean exit.
  The per-cycle hci_reset at discovery start also clears it. So a clean shutdown
  is covered; the gap is a CRASH leaving stale entries the kernel keeps trying
  to auto-connect, lingering into the next run.
- Refinement (optional, cleaner than a power-cycle just to clear the list):
    1. A BlueHydra::Mgmt helper issuing mgmt Remove Device with the all-zero
       BDADDR (00:00:00:00:00:00, addr_type 0x00) = "remove ALL" - one command,
       no power-cycle.
    2. Call it once at startup to drop stale entries from a prior crashed run.
    3. Optionally use it in Runner#stop instead of the shutdown hci_reset if the
       full power-cycle proves heavier than needed just to clear the list.
- Constraints: discovery thread owns auto_connect_list; do removal there / via
  the mgmt instance. No DB access. Verify with btmgmt that the list is empty
  after exit and after a startup clear following a simulated crash.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11. Improve the drain_connection_events workflow (stop blunt-clearing the queue)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Background: process_connection_events prunes the auto-connect list on mgmt
  Device Disconnected/Connect Failed - the point of the event-driven design is
  to remove a device the moment its connect/interrogate/disconnect cycle
  completes, so the kernel's ACTION_AUTO_CONNECT stops re-connecting it.
- Wart: connect_phase starts with drain_connection_events, a blunt
  connection_events.clear. Now that scan_phase also processes events, that clear
  THROWS AWAY Disconnected/Failed events that landed at the scan/connect boundary
  for devices we still track, delaying their removal until clear_auto_connect at
  phase end - the churn the design is meant to avoid.
- Not-obviously-safe to just delete the clear: addresses recur across cycles, so
  a stale :connected from a prior cycle could be mis-attributed to a re-added
  entry for the same address. process_connection_events already ignores
  untracked addresses (next unless entry), so stale events for gone devices are
  no-ops - same-address recurrence is the case to handle.
- Direction: drop the blunt clear and rely on the existing filter PLUS a
  per-cycle guard against same-address staleness (stamp entries with an
  added_at/generation token and ignore events enqueued before the entry was
  (re-)added); or a targeted purge of only untracked-address events. Process
  events continuously (scan AND connect phases) so the queue never needs wiping.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
12. Rate-limit LE Read Remote Version to the info-scan cadence
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Background: BlueHydra::HciCommand issues a Read Remote Version on EVERY LE
  Connection Complete. That is what populated LE versions, and it works, but it
  fires far more than needed: ACTION_AUTO_CONNECT reconnects a device on each
  re-advertise and info_scan_rate re-queues it, so a device connects many times
  per run (observed: 11 devices, 50 connections, 48 version reads). Once we have
  a good version, re-reading on every reconnect is wasted HCI traffic in the
  already-tiny connected window.
- Goal: fire the version read no more often than the info-scan cadence for that
  device - gate it like an info scan (info_scan_rate + item 9 success-gating)
  instead of once per connection.
- Design: gate per device (by address), not per handle. The hard part is the
  thread boundary - HciCommand runs on its own raw-HCI thread, only knows the
  HANDLE, and must not touch the DB. Preferred: parse the peer address out of
  the LE (Enhanced) Connection Complete event (offsets after the handle) and
  consult a small in-memory address -> last-version-read map (shares the handle
  map of item 3b). PRESERVE THE RETRY BENEFIT: only start the cooldown once a
  device has a GOOD version (real LMP, not 0x00/0xff); until then keep retrying
  on each reconnect (that is what rescues flaky devices whose read races the
  disconnect). Consider folding info scan / l2ping / version read into one shared
  per-device scan cadence (with item 9).
- BlueHydra::ConnectTracker is now the obvious home for that shared per-device
  state: it already holds an in-memory, mutex-guarded, address-keyed entry
  written by the result thread and read by the discovery thread, which is exactly
  the thread boundary this item describes as the hard part. Adding a
  last-version-read stamp (and item 9's last-success stamp) to that entry avoids
  inventing a third registry, and it inherits the forget-on-offline behaviour for
  free. Note it is deliberately NOT persisted; if any of this state needs to
  survive a restart, that is a separate decision.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
13. Finish the connect-attempt gating (connectability + strikes)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Landed: a connect_to_nonconnectable config (default false) that skips devices
  which have advertised themselves non-connectable and never advertised
  otherwise, plus an unconditional 3-consecutive-failure strike rule. Both live
  in BlueHydra::ConnectTracker, in memory only, consulted from request_leinfo so
  they cover the kernel auto-connect path and the direct-connect path together.
  Strikes come from direct-connect unreachable/error, auto-connect Connect
  Failed, and auto-connect timeouts; they clear on any successful connect and are
  dropped when Device.mark_offline runs. Measured basis: over a 47 hour run, 733
  of 1329 attempted addresses had never advertised connectable (~27% of attempts
  wasted), and one address burned 107 attempts for zero successes.
- Remaining, and the biggest piece: MODE-SPECIFIC STRIKES WITH THEIR OWN BACKOFF.
  Both LE and classic should keep strikes, independently per mode, on a backoff
  timer that is not info_scan_rate. Two changes, and they belong together:
  * Key strikes on (address, MODE), not on address alone. Today ConnectTracker
    keys on address and every strike comes from an LE connect, which is exactly
    why push_to_queue has to special-case `mode == :le` before applying the fast
    retry - without that guard a dual-mode device's LE failures would suppress and
    re-cadence its classic scans, which an LE failure is no evidence for. Keying
    per mode deletes that special case and lets the two paths hold independent
    streaks for the same device: a phone that refuses LE connects but answers a
    classic page (or the reverse) gets written off on one transport only. Expect
    per-mode STRIKE_LIMIT too - a classic page is slower and more expensive than
    an LE connect, so the ceilings and intervals probably should not match.
  * Give the failure path its own timer, separate from info_scan_rate. Today
    FAILED_RETRY_INTERVAL is consulted INSIDE push_to_queue's info_scan_rate gate
    and shares its query_history stamp, so "how often do we refresh a device we
    already have data for" and "how long do we wait after a failed connect" are
    entangled in one number written in one place. They are different things and
    should be measured separately: hold a per-(address, mode) last_failure_at in
    ConnectTracker and back off on its own schedule, ideally progressive across
    the strikes (e.g. 15s / 30s / 60s) rather than one flat interval, so a
    genuinely flaky device gets a fair third attempt without a hopeless one being
    retried hard. Once that exists the failure path neither reads nor writes
    query_history, and info_scan_rate goes back to meaning only what its name says.
  * Classic's failure signal has to come from somewhere first. The classic info
    scan reports a subprocess stderr string (hcitool info) rather than an mgmt
    event, so the cleanest sequencing is after item 4 replaces it with our own
    HCI reads and classic failures become mgmt/HCI statuses like the LE paths.
    In the meantime two usable signals already exist: RESET_WORTHY_CONNECT_ERROR
    / the stderr matching in scan_with_reset_retry, and BlueHydra::L2Ping's
    outcome, which is already a clean reachable/unreachable. Either could seed
    classic strikes before item 4 lands.
  * Connectability does NOT generalise: classic devices do not advertise, so there
    is no equivalent signal and the connect_to_nonconnectable gate stays LE-only.
    Only the strike half of this item is cross-mode.
  * :abandoned deliberately does not strike (budget ran out, no evidence about
    the device). Revisit if abandoned counts ever climb - a device that is always
    abandoned is never actually evaluated, which is its own bug.
  * No CUI visibility. ConnectTracker counts nonconnectable_skipped and
    struck_out_skipped and logs each skip at debug, but nothing is on the debug
    CUI line next to the auto-connect / direct-LE counters. Add it when the next
    on-device run needs to show the gates are working without grepping a log.
  * The "advertised both ways" case is treated as connectable on purpose, so a
    dual-mode advertiser that never connects is caught by strikes rather than by
    the connectability gate. If a real device turns out to advertise connectable
    and non-connectable in alternation while genuinely refusing connections, the
    stricter "ever sent non-connectable" rule is the fallback - it will suppress
    more, including some devices that would have answered.
  * Unbounded-growth check: the tracker holds one small entry per address seen,
    dropped on offline. Private addresses rotate, so the set churns; confirm on a
    long run that tracked_count tracks the online device count rather than
    climbing forever.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
14. Parsed-value fidelity: malformed proximity UUID, and numbers stored as text
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- All found while splitting the four things btmon labels "TX power:" apart. That
  fix landed, and so did the proximity UUID grouping below; what is left changes a
  stored format and needs deciding rather than doing.
- LANDED: le_proximity_uuid was misgrouped, one missing dash. The old pattern
  scan(/(.{8})(.{4})(.{4})(.*)/) had FOUR capture groups where a UUID has five, so
  the trailing 16 hex characters came out as one blob:
    wire bytes:  74278bdab64445208f0c720eaf059935
    was:         74278bda-b644-4520-8f0c720eaf059935   (8-4-4-16)
    now:         74278bda-b644-4520-8f0c-720eaf059935  (8-4-4-4-12)
  It affected 64 of 64 iBeacon chunks in a 9 minute capture, i.e. every sighting.
  The byte reversal around it is CORRECT and verified byte for byte against a raw
  payload - bluez prints this UUID reversed from the wire (print_manufacturer_apple
  reads it back to front with get_le32/get_le16 per group) - so do not "fix" that
  part. Parser#proximity_uuid now also returns nil rather than a partial string,
  because an empty UUID is an identity key two unrelated beacons could collide on.
- Consequences of that change, accepted deliberately rather than migrated:
  * Existing rows keep the old shape until each beacon is seen again.
    Device.update_or_create_from_result matches on
    (le_proximity_uuid, le_major_num, le_minor_num) when an address has rotated,
    so a beacon already in the DB is seen as a new device once, then settles.
  * Worth knowing where this value goes, since it is not obvious from
    syncable_attributes: le_proximity_uuid is not in that list, but the list is only
    the change-gated tier. sync_to_pulse and stream_builder_data send the beacon
    triple unconditionally whenever it is set, outside that loop, because the cloud
    matches records on it (item 15). So this is a key on the far side, not a display
    string - worth remembering if its format is ever touched again.
  * No migration needed for the regrouping itself. The cloud store was empty when
    this landed, so there were no records keyed on the old shape, and locally
    update_or_create_from_result tries address BEFORE the triple - a static-address
    beacon is found by address and has its le_proximity_uuid updated in place, same
    record, same sync_id. Only a rotating-address beacon already in the local DB
    gets a second record, once.
  * SETTLED: le_ibeacon_measured_power is synced, ibeacon_range is not.
    The measured power is a per-beacon calibration constant, so it goes clean after
    the first sighting and the change gate never resends it - and it is the half of
    the distance calculation nothing downstream can get another way. The range is a
    pure function of it and the RSSI, both on the wire, so sending it would ship a
    derived value and freeze the free-space path loss exponent (2) that this
    calculates with, which we already know reads long indoors. Anything downstream
    can derive distance from the whole le_rssi series with an exponent of its
    choosing. The column stays for the CUI's range field.
  * While reading that: syncable_attributes is a misleading name. It is the
    change-gated subset, not "everything we sync". Three attributes go every time
    (uuid as sync_id, status, address), three more go whenever set
    (le_proximity_uuid, le_major_num, le_minor_num), and le_company_data/company
    go unconditionally when both are set DESPITE also being in the list. Only six
    properties are genuinely never sent: id, uap_lap, created_at, updated_at,
    ibeacon_range, le_ibeacon_measured_power. Renaming it, or folding the
    always-send tier into one declared structure, would stop the next reader
    concluding what this one did - that absence from the list means absence from
    the wire.
- Numeric attributes are stored as display strings, so duplicates resolve by
  LEXICAL sort. le_tx_power holds "3 dBm"; the normal-attribute loop in
  Device.update_or_create_from_result picks result[attr].uniq.sort.first, which
  ranked "127 dBm" above "3 dBm" because "1" < "3". Dropping the 127 sentinel
  removed the case that mattered, but the ordering is still wrong for any device
  that legitimately reports two different powers, and the same applies to
  classic_tx_power and le_ibeacon_measured_power.
  * Minimum fix: sort these numerically (to_i) rather than lexically.
  * Better: store the integer dBm and render the unit at the display/sync edge.
    That is a schema and sync-format change, hence its own item. ibeacon_range is
    already the odd one out - a Float from the parser into a String column, which
    is why it needed explicit last-value handling instead of the shared loop.
- Related and unresolved: ibeacon_range uses free-space path loss (exponent 2).
  Indoors the exponent is nearer 2-4, so the estimate reads long - 14-71m for
  RSSI -73..-85 against a -59 dB reference in the capture above. Now that the
  estimate is actually produced and persisted, it is worth deciding whether that
  formula is good enough to show in the CUI's range column or needs a calibrated
  exponent.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
15. Sync payload: the two features bitmaps are shaped wrong on the wire
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Both found by inspecting a real payload from Device#stream_builder_data rather
  than by reading, and both now FIXED. Kept here because each changed the shape on
  the wire, which was an accepted decision rather than a silent one.
- LANDED: le_features_bitmap and classic_features_bitmap were sent as JSON STRINGS
  while their nine siblings were sent as parsed structures:
    le_features         wire type Array    ["LE Encryption"]
    le_flags            wire type Array    ["LE General Discoverable Mode"]
    le_rssi             wire type Array    [{"t"=>1,"rssi"=>"-50 dBm"}]
    le_features_bitmap  wire type String   "{\"0\":\"0x1f\"}"   <- was
    classic_features_bitmap  wire type String   "{\"0\":\"0xff\"}"   <- was
  sync_to_pulse and stream_builder_data branch on is_serialized?, and these two
  were missing from that list even though their setters JSON.generate like the rest
  and they sit in the same "update array attributes" list in
  update_or_create_from_result. A consumer had to double-parse exactly these two of
  the eleven. They are in the list now, so both arrive as objects.
- LANDED: the empty-value skip guard missed them for the same reason. It read
  `unless [nil, "[]"].include?(val)`, which assumes array-backed storage; these two
  are hash-backed and serialize to "{}" when empty:
    le_features         empty -> stored "[]"  -> omitted from payload   (correct)
    le_features_bitmap  empty -> stored "{}"  -> SENT as the string "{}"
  Now EMPTY_SYNC_VALUES, consulted through empty_for_sync? by both payload
  builders, so the two copies of that loop cannot disagree about what empty means -
  this bug was in both of them.
- SETTLED, will not do: created_at stays local. The cloud keeps data forever while
  the local DB is cleared periodically, so its own first-sighting record is both
  more complete than ours and the one that matters; sending ours would only
  contradict it after a local wipe.
- SETTLED, leave as is: le_proximity_uuid, le_major_num and le_minor_num are sent
  on EVERY sync rather than change-gated, from the always-send block in both sync
  methods. That is three redundant fields per message for an immutable triple, and
  it is correct - the cloud matches records on that triple (intended design), so it
  is an identity key and has to be present on every message, exactly like address.
  Moving it into syncable_attributes would send it once and then stop, and every
  later message would be unmatchable. Do not "optimise" this.
  * Related: this is why item 14's regrouping of le_proximity_uuid is a change to a
    key rather than to a display string. It needed no migration because the cloud
    store was empty when it landed, but the next change to that format will not get
    that for free.

